referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int[] chainTurntableProofStrip(ListNode head, int k) {
    ListNode a=head; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {ListNode previous=null,node=a;while(node!=null){ListNode next=node.next;node.next=previous;previous=node;node=next;}for(node=previous;node!=null;node=node.next)out.add(node.val);}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Reverse the links in the linked list given by head. Each node has a number in val and a link in next. After the links are reversed, follow the new list from start to end. Record its values in that order. An empty list gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Return the whole result list in the order you built it. If it is empty, return [].

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

